                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Local multiplayer was kinda cool. I only had one friend to play it with but it was quite fun, and I can imagine that if you manage to get a few people together it would be a fun blast.\n\nHOWEVER, I tried to play online early on, and there was nobody around, the online game never started.  If the online server had some bots to let you spawn into, that would be kinda cool, but sadly it doesn't seem to, so there's only a blank screen to stare at.\n\nThe graphics are excellent, massive kudos to whoever drew the levels.  Excellent bullet  animations and first-rate lighting and sound design.   If this were a single-player game, it would feel really cool and old school.\n\nHOWEVER, it's primarily multi-player skirmish, and as such it does get very repetitive.  We found a couple of loadouts that seemed to be much better than others, and once we settled on them, we found that the game became a bit samey.\n\nAs it is, I'd give it a neutral, perhaps negative review, PURELY because it's very limited in how much entertainment you can get from it, which is a shame because it's clearly a very good engine with a lot of excellent thought gone into it.\n\nIf they could take out the multiplayer stuff, and convert it to a cool sci-fi cyberpunk style platformer, perhaps with co-op play, that would be awesome.\n\nI don't know what to give it... Steam REALLY needs a \"neutral\", because I feel bad giving this a negative review, since it's so well crafted.  However, due to the lack of community, coupled with some glitches and freezes while attempting to play online, and the limited appeal of offline play, I'm going to have to edge towards a negative.   This doesn't reflect on the work that went into this though.    \n\nIf you're looking for a fun little PC-based brawler for 2 or more people to play at a LAN party, it's a good laugh... for a little while...\n\n",
